<section class="scoring-explainer" id="consistent-scoring" aria-labelledby="scoring-explainer-title">
  <div class="launch-section-heading">
    <div>
      <p class="ux-page-eyebrow">A common basis for comparison</p>
      <h2 id="scoring-explainer-title">Consistent evaluation and scoring</h2>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="explainer-lead">
    Reported errors depend on the test split, spatial sampling, normalisation and aggregation procedure. FluidsBench specifies these choices for each
    benchmark to support reproducible evaluation and meaningful comparisons between models.
  </p>
  <div class="scoring-principles">
    <article>
      <span>01 / Evaluation protocol</span>
      <h3>Common test conditions</h3>
      <p>
        Models are evaluated on the same required cases and locations within a dataset and split, even when they use different meshes or
        representations.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <span>02 / Metric definitions</span>
      <h3>Complementary physical quantities</h3>
      <p>
        Where supported, fields, integrated forces and flow profiles test complementary aspects of the physics. Each dataset publishes its metrics,
        weights and score calculation.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <span>03 / Result interpretation</span>
      <h3>Aggregate and component metrics</h3>
      <p>
        The overall score summarises performance under the published weighting. Individual metrics and profile comparisons expose differences in field
        accuracy, integrated quantities and local flow structure.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <aside class="scoring-reading-note">
    <strong>Interpreting normalised scores</strong>
    <p>
      Higher overall scores are better on the published 0–100 scale. A score of 90 does not mean 90% accuracy. Compare models within the same dataset
      and split; scores across different benchmarks are not interchangeable. Raw metrics remain available alongside the summary.
    </p>
  </aside>
  <div class="tour-methodology-links">
    <a href="{{ '/datasets/drivaerml/' | relative_url }}#overall-score">DrivAerML scoring formula and weights →</a>
    <a href="{{ '/datasets/drivaerml/' | relative_url }}#metric-definitions">DrivAerML metric definitions →</a>
    <a href="{{ '/datasets/' | relative_url }}">All dataset evaluation guides →</a>
  </div>
</section>
